  "result": "The 10-minute lesson on O3 is written: `lessons/0001-context-and-memory.html`. I haven't opened it in a browser or counted its words. I aimed for roughly 1,500 words by section budget.\n\nIt covers three concepts, each opening with a makerspace example, then the name, a \"Why this works\" box and a check:\n1. **Context window:** the model keeps nothing between steps, so your program re-sends the whole message list on every call. That list grows, and you pay for it each time.\n2. **Notes:** a student's training status is saved to a file and loaded into the next session, because the context is rebuilt from scratch each session.\n3. **Retrieval:** search the 200-page manual and read one page instead of pasting it all in.\n\nIt also has a warm-up recall question, a short worked example and practice task, and a summary. A \"common mix-up\" box covers the idea that a bigger window means you can skip retrieval.\n\nCompaction, meaning summarizing a session whose context has filled up, is left for a later lesson. The primary source is Anthropic's [Effective context engineering for AI agents](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents), and the other cited source is the [Claude context windows docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows). I also added `RESOURCES.md` and a line in `NOTES.md`.\n\nI wrote no cheat sheet or learning record, since nothing has been shown yet. The practice questions don't reuse anything from quiz 0001, and they leave out its answers.\n\nAsk for a quiz on this lesson when you're ready, or ask me anything about it.\n\nI couldn't find a community for testing these skills, so the lesson has none. Would you like me to look for one?",
